OK, then you have to turn off simplification and use `ratsimp()` instead of `rat()` : 
```text
simp: false;


rationalize(1/3*x+1/2*y+5/6*x+0.75*y);
```

Another simple idea is to convert one random variable (one index) into d variables (d indices): 
```text
/* Function: unflat_index (row-major order)
   Purpose: Convert flat index 'k' into multidimensional index [i1, i2, ..., id]
   Input:
     k    - flat index (integer, starting from 1)
     dims - list of dimension sizes [n1, n2, ..., nd]
   Output:
     list of coordinates [i1, i2, ..., id] */
unflat_index(k, dims) := block([coords: [], size, idx],
    k: k - 1, /* shift to 0-based indexing */
    /* Work from last dimension backwards */
    for size in reverse(dims) do (
        idx: mod(k, size) + 1,
        coords: cons(idx, coords),
        k: floor(k / size)
    ),

    return(coords)
);
```
 And try this: 
```text
var1: [A, B, C]; /* rows */
var2: [1, 2]; /* columns */

k: stack_seed; /* rand(length(var1)*length(var2)) + 1 */
[i,j]: unflat_index(k, [length(var1),length(var2)]);
vars: [var1[i], var2[j]];
```
 Then you can deploy `length(var1)*length(var2)` variants

