Jerry, I think what you may be trying to do is to apply the "rule" sqrt(x)*sqrt(x) -> x. If so, then the following code might help.  You need to start a Maxima session, from the command line. 
```text
simp:false;


p1: (2 + sqrt (2)) * sqrt (2);


p2:distrib(p1);


p3:sqrt(a)*sqrt(b)*sqrt(b)*sqrt(b)*sqrt(a)*1*sqrt(b)+1;
```
 The first line turns off all simplification. Have you come across this in Maxima? The variable p2 is p1 "expanded out". Now, I've defined some functions which implement the rule sqrt(x)*sqrt(x) -> x recursively they make use of a STACK function 
```text
safe_op(ex) := block(


  if mapatom(ex) then return(""),


  if stringp(op(ex)) then return(op(ex)) else return(string(op(ex)))


)$
```
 This returns the "operator" of an expression as a string.  This is not core Maxima, but is in the STACK library. Try the following functions: 
```text
naivesqrt(ex):=block([al],


  if atom(ex) then return(ex),


  al:args(ex),
```
 E.g. try 
```text
naivesqrt(p1);


naivesqrt(p2);


naivesqrt(p3);
```
 Maxima has two modes of working 
```text
simp:true;


simp:false;
```
 and unfortunately there is little by way of control between these two extremes, i.e. either "simplify everything" or "simplify nothing".  I've been aware for a while that some functions in-between would be useful.  A couple of years ago I started to create a more systematic package of functions to give this level of fine control.  See https://github.com/maths/moodle-qtype_stack/blob/master/stack/maxima/elementary.mac This is currently not connected to the main STACK codebase (so you won't have these functions available on your webserver). It would also be useful to have incorrect rules!  These would help in generating an expression which arises from a common mistake, which we need to do if we randomly generate questions.  There are some examples in the above file. I didn't get round to adding the rule you asked for to this collection, hence the bespoke code above. So, it is possible.  I'm not quite sure how to proceed with all this, which is one reason the coding stalled.  Suggestions would be welcome. Chris

Eduardo, Thank you for your kind comments about STACK. I hope you and your students find this a useful tool as part of their teaching. I agree that we should try to solve as many mathematical problems as possible at the CAS level.  While it would be easy to write \(@n@:@m@\) in the question, this isn't really very elegant. For reasons I'm sure you appreciated, I have restricted the Maxima functions available to teachers, and drastically restricted those available to students.  I don't want teachers in general to be able to create new functions because this could be a security flaw, so "infix" is not allowed in private static $teachernotallow on line 104 of https://github.com/maths/moodle-qtype_stack/blob/master/stack/cas/casstring.class.php I do want to support features where there is a good case though. I suggest you get some code working in Maxima which you want, then we can talk about how to include it either locally in your version of STACK or in the core. You could use the function "ratio" (which is not defined in STACK) to represent this. For example, in a maxima application you could simply start by defining texput(ratio,":",infix); The function ratio has not been defined, so this is a function which does not evaluate, i.e. at this stage it is just a noun which represents something. If you want to perform division in a later calculation then you can substitute the "ratio" function for division.  Try the following: 
```text
texput(ratio,":",infix);


p:ratio(n,m);


tex(p);


subst(ratio="/",p);
```
 Of course, texput is also not permitted by teachers by default either!  If this is all you need, you could add it to stackmaxima.mac in your local version. Does this make sense? If you want to change all division operators to use :, simply use 
```text
texput("/",":",infix);
```
