 I have to be moved to the top of the page. I have modified your file slightly and attached the revised version. I do not think we should use the <math> or <mathml> tags for the TeX to mathml conversion. There are two reasons: 1) While I agree that TeX is the standard mathematical format, in that virtually all mathematical literature is written in this format, it is not the only mathematical markup out there and thus should not use the generic <math> tag. 2) I think that once people are starting to use MathML enabled webpages then the textfilter should read a cookie to determine whether to convert to HTML or to MathML. This should not be fixed by the tag. The attached modified version already has those <math> and <mathml> tags removed.

Our Moodle here at the University of York is now serving MathML. As you may know, MathML is an XML -based markup language for publishing mathematics on the Web. So this is an example of Moodle serving XML compliant pages. Many thanks to Martin for his many changes making it possible to switch short tags off, which was a prerequisite to using XML. Mad Alex has been thinking carefully about how to integrate the possibility of having XML pages into Moodle without upsetting users who have no need for XML. He has asked me to post his solution here. It consists of having and extra optional argument $usexml to the function print_header in weblib.php. The function print_header is used by most Moodle pages to produce the page header. For XML pages the header needs to contains all kinds of extra information. If the optional argument $usexml is set to true then print_header produces this header information. This is done by having the following extra code in the definition of print_header: 
```text
    if ($usexml) {


        @header("Content-type: text/xml");


        echo "<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"no\" ?>\n";


        if ($CFG->xml_stylesheets) {


            $stylesheets = explode(";", $CFG->xml_stylesheets);


            foreach ($stylesheets as $stylesheet) {


                echo "<?xml-stylesheet type=\"text/xsl\" href=\"$CFG->
wwwroot
/$stylesheet\" ?>\n";


            }


        }


        echo "<!DOCTYPE html PUBLIC \"-//W3C//DTD 
XHTML
 1.1";


        if ($CFG->xml_doctype_extra)


            echo " plus $CFG->xml_doctype_extra";


        echo "//" . strtoupper(current_language()) . "\" \"$CFG->xml_dtd\">\n";
```
 The advantage of using this extra optional argument to determine whether to write an XML header is that only those pages that need XML will have to be XML compliant. In this way the transition to an XML compliant Moodle can be done slowly and smoothly. Because different sites may want to use XML for different purposes and different kinds of XML need different kinds of information in the header, the function takes this information from extra entries in the config table in Moodle's database . For us these entries are: name:                    value: 
```text
xml_doctype_extra     MathML 2.0




xml_stylesheets       lib/t2mml/mathml.xsl


xml_dtd               
http://www.w3.org/Math/DTD/mathml2/xhtml-math11-f.dtd
```
 Attached to this post is a zip file with a modified lib/weblib.php, theme/standard/header.html and theme/standard/footer.html. I have also included unix diff files for these, working off version 1.154 for weblib and equally recent header and footer files. I have not included full context diffs because except for the above bit of code all the changes are trivial corrections of non-xml compliant html. I hope that Martin can incorporate these changes in CVS . Anyone can then use Moodle to create XML pages by calling print_header with the extra argument $usexml set to true. The changes won't change anything for people who do not need XML.
